{"study_accession":"PRJNA781245","secondary_study_accession":"SRP346600","title":"Divergent spatial microdomains drive inflammation and repair in Ulcerative and Immune Checkpoint Therapy Colitis - CD3 scRNA-Seq","description":"Adult inflammatory bowel disease is incompletely understood. We combine unbiased single-cell RNA sequencing (gene expression profiling, CITE-seq derived cell surface protein data, TCR and BCR sequence data) with unbiased spatial transcriptomics to interrogate changes across immune and non-immune populations in colitis and health, across tissue and blood. We compare idiopathic ulcerative colitis with hitherto under-studied immune checkpoint therapy induced colitis, utilizing non-inflamed disease states as additional controls. We identify patterns of inflammation and response unique and common to both diseases, and infer changes in cell trafficking with potential therapeutic implications. We go on to localize disease-specific changes in tissue using spatial transcriptomics. We leverage this data to interrogate cellular interactions in an unbiased manner, allowing us to describe novel microdomains of inflammation and repair. Overall design: Flow-cytometry (FACS) sorted CD3 CD45 double positive T cells from paired tissue and blood, in patients with active ulcerative colitis (UC_I) or active immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced colitis (CC_I), compared with healthy controls (HC). Patients with CC_I were either treated with anti-PD-1 immunotherapy (Monotherapy) or a combination of simultaneous anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA4 immunotherapy (Dual therapy).","pubmed":"38744246","geo_series":"","n_samples":15,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN23243558","study_accession":"PRJNA781245","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"Pool 22 TCR: Sorted CD3_TCR_Blood_Pool19_Repeat 2","description":"Pool 22 TCR: Sorted CD3_TCR_Blood_Pool19_Repeat 2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM5693658","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":4,"first_public":"2024-04-14","at